{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Blessed be He who enlarges Gad.</b> This teaches that the territory of Gad extended toward the east — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:20), from the Sifrei. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that because He likens him to a lion, He said, 'Blessed be He who enlarges Gad,' for He gave him might and all who see him flee from him, and there is none who can contend with him. But the correct interpretation is that it refers to his inheritance, for the two tribes that took their inheritance across the Jordan did not take it by lot, but rather Moses gave them the entire kingdom of Sihon and Og, and their inheritance was broad and extended above all the tribes. And He did not mention this concerning Reuben, for his prayer suffices, and it is also possible that the children of Gad took more than the children of Reuben, because they were warriors and He gave them the entire region, and the cities they built were more numerous than those of Reuben. And furthermore, they were the ones who sought that land first, as I explained there (Numbers 32:2), and so they are the cause of this enlargement."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The idiom מי יצר לו is rendered literally as 'who can contend with him'; it could also mean 'who can oppose him.' The reference to Numbers 32:2 is Ramban's own commentary there."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:44.163171917Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}